IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `duplicate-type-a`
IMPORT `duplicate-type-b`

-- Two imported modules each declare a `Thing`, and this module's exported
-- decisions reach both. `l4 check` SUCCEEDS: they are distinct entities and
-- every use resolves to the right one.
--
-- The emitted Catala has one flat namespace, and the scan that builds the
-- artifact deduplicates structures by their L4 name. Measured on the binary
-- that read the import closure but did not yet check this — exit 0, and:
--
--     declaration structure Thing:
--       data n content decimal
--     …
--       equals Thing { -- n: x }
--     …
--       equals Thing { -- m: x }
--
-- one declaration, two incompatible construction sites, nothing said. The
-- existing collision check cannot see it: it reports when DISTINCT L4 names
-- mangle to one Catala name, and here the L4 names are equal.
--
-- Refused instead (§8.1.2). Only declarations the artifact needs are checked —
-- two modules in a large closure may both declare something the emitted code
-- never reaches, and refusing that would make importing a module a liability.

@export reaches the first `Thing`
GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
top x MEANS (`from a` x)'s n

@export reaches the second one
GIVEN x IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
other x MEANS (`from b` x)'s m
